're being real  here with ourselves guys you can  probably go out and buy a $20 stylist no  problems whatsoever  because the difference between a first  generation Apple pencil and any hold  stylist off the block besides  static appearances there isn't really  too much I think the main point of  difference for me personally is the  simple fact of how the Apple pencil  charges yes it's not the prettiest thing  in the world  but every other example that you look at  be at Logitech Wacom or again a wish  product that's real cheap and nasty they  all come with the similar charging  cables that you actually have to plug  them in and plug them into a wall  charger whereas with the Apple pencil  you can simply just plug it into the  bottom of the Apple device and it will  charge on the go or just sitting at your  desk  I think this relatively simple feature  that's been implemented into the Apple  pencil first generation at least is  something that kind of persuades people  to purchase it when they do start  looking into alternatives for the Apple  pencil if on the other hand you're  comparing an Apple second-generation  pencil to potentially any other stylist  out there in the market I think you  really have to reconsider your choices  by going out and looking at a different  option with the Apple second-generation  pencil you have all the cons from the  first generation fixed up and made 10  times better  there's no more little lid at the end  that comes off and you can lose very  quickly very easily it's very flimsy and  it's not very fantastic and you also  don't have this simple rolling problem  when you lay it flat it comes with a  flat edge on one side which allows you  to place it simply on a surface or  magnetically connected to the iPad Pro  and charge at the same time  so overall guys what's my opinion on  these Apple pencils I think yes they are  completely overpriced products I don't  think they're warrant the 200 Australian  dollar price day that they come with but  at the same time I don't think there is  a competitor out there that is even  close to having all the features and all  the little bells and whistles that the  Apple pencil one and the Apple pencil to  come with so if you're somebody out  there that is looking to take notes  every single day looking to draw  potentially every single day or edit in  apps like Lightroom Photoshop  Illustrator anything like that that  requires finesse and pinpoint precision  then I think  you should definitely go out and invest  in an Apple pencil if you're just an  average user doing some quick emails  potentially watching some YouTube videos  then I don't think you really need to go  out and spend the additional $200 on an  Apple pencil when you can go out and  spend 20 30 $40 on your generic stylus  for the iPad and even in that situation  most of the time I don't actually  believe that a stylus is required for me  personally I do a lot of architecture so  I have to do lots of measurements lots  of drawings lots of markups on pages and  having a pencil is a very useful tool  whereas if I was just doing a general  day-to-day tasks you wouldn't really  need a stylus to work properly and you'd  probably find it less productive for  most people and that's all I have for  you guys today thank you so much for  watching if you enjoyed the video smash  that subscribe button  20-19 style and as always I'll see you  next Monday

